# Gate step 1252-znbn — data, not code (order 1072-b7eq).
#
# WHAT IT GUARDS. run-litmus-test.sh opens a critical_path item ONLY on
# `- step: "..."`. An item opened by any other key — `- name:`, measured on
# macuahuitl 2026-09-18 — matches no branch, falls through the parser's elif
# chain, and its `command:`/`timeout_ms:`/`expected_behavior:` keys OVERWRITE
# the step already in progress. The item merges BACKWARDS into its predecessor,
# later keys win, and a two-item file yields one unlabelled step that can never
# pass. `- step:` with an unquoted name falls through identically.
#
# WHY NOTHING CAUGHT IT. Every observable said fine. The YAML is well-formed,
# so ./build.sh --check's metadata validator passes the file. The runner prints
# a step count that silently omits the merged item and exits 0. MEASURED on the
# pre-fix runner against a two-item file:
#
#     ok:litmus-parseable:<file>:1 step(s)      rc=0
#
# Two items in, one step out, green. A step that was LOST and a step that was
# NEVER WRITTEN produce identical output, so no reader downstream — human or
# gate — can tell them apart. That is why this needs a guard and not a
# convention about how to write YAML.
#
# THE TEETH ARE ARMS 1 AND 3. Arm 1 pins the refusal and requires it to NAME the
# offending key, because "parse error" alone sends the author looking at their
# YAML syntax, which is valid. Arm 3 is the mutation control and it was RUN, not
# assumed: it neuters the collection line, reruns the same two-item file, and
# requires the pre-fix `1 step(s)` / exit 0 to come back. Arms 2 and 4 are
# regression guards — the unquoted-name case, and a valid two-item file still
# parsing as exactly 2 steps.
#
# ARM 4 ASSERTS THE COUNT, NOT THE EXIT CODE, deliberately. The defect being
# guarded produces a GREEN exit with a wrong count, so an arm checking only rc
# would pass against the very bug it exists to catch.
#
# ARM 3 FAILS LOUDLY WHEN IT CANNOT MUTATE rather than skipping. Its first
# version used `sed 's|...|...|'` on a line that itself contains `|`; the
# substitution silently did nothing and the arm reported that it had proved
# nothing. A mutation arm that cannot mutate must refuse, or it is a green light
# for a guard that was never exercised.
#
# SAFE BY MEASUREMENT, NOT BY ASSUMPTION. Across openspec/litmus-tests: 435
# files, 2514 critical_path items, ALL opened by `- step:` and ALL with
# double-quoted names — so the refusal cannot redden the existing corpus. The
# whole corpus was then run through `--parse-only` on the pre-fix and post-fix
# runners and the verdict lines are BYTE-IDENTICAL, all 423 ok files and every
# step count, with the same 12 pre-existing failures (9 no-parseable-steps,
# 3 command-not-extractable) unchanged. This step adds a refusal for a shape the
# corpus does not currently contain.
#
# COST, MEASURED HERE 2026-09-18: 0.24 / 0.25 / 0.24 s. It is parse-only — no
# compile, no container, no network. That median is inside the fast-refusal
# lane's admission rule (under one second, order 1009-gccx) and this step would
# be a reasonable candidate to move there, where it would refuse before any
# compile rather than after; it is placed in the ordinary lane here only because
# moving it is a separate change to a different file.
#
# STEP_SCRIPT is a LITERAL path on purpose: a binding assembled from a
# variable is invisible to every name-based scan, including the
# bound-or-retired guard (1063-nraf). Keep it a plain string.
STEP_DESC="Checking a litmus critical_path item cannot merge into its predecessor (1252-znbn)"
STEP_SCRIPT="scripts/test-litmus-item-opener-refused.sh"
STEP_ERROR="a critical_path item opened by a key other than 'step:' merges backwards into the previous step and the file silently loses a step (1252-znbn) — read which arm failed above; arm 3 failing means the guard itself is no longer exercised"
STEP_OK="Litmus critical_path items cannot merge silently"
